    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2fee6712_414d2012",
        "filename": "bench/CompositingImagesBench.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 228,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-19T14:35:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This (and the very similar explicit flushes in the other benchmarks and Viewer) will break timing when running on the graphite backend.\n\nGraphite\u0027s timings had been relying on `surface-\u003eflush()` to get down to to the `Surface_Graphite::onFlush`, which has now been moved to `skgpu::graphite::Flush` that is never called. This will bias the viewer reported stats by pushing more work to the final flushAndSubmit that isn\u0027t wrapped in any timing, and for benchmarks, it\u0027ll mean that clears at the start of a loop will just delete the previously recorded commands and so we will think graphite is super fast.\n\nSince these functions are no-ops when the surface doesn\u0027t fit, I think it would make sense to add \n\n    #if defined(SK_GRAPHITE)\n        skgpu::graphite::Flush(canvas-\u003egetSurface());\n    #endif\n    \n as well, although that gets pretty verbose. Is it possible to have a single `skgpu::Flush(SkSurface*)` that does the right thing for both graphite and ganesh while still meeting the modular goals? It wouldn\u0027t have to be a public API, it\u0027s really just to help migration for tests, viewer, and nanobench.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 226,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 228,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "f6e80a1ec29eb6c40bdc7dc38c4e357e9ea1058a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
